1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:01,670
Time for our new family member to come out. Come on out.

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:04,610
- Hello. - Sung Hanbin of ZEROBASEONE.

3
00:00:05,010 --> 00:00:07,010
The theme is Street Food.

4
00:00:07,110 --> 00:00:08,710
My hometown is Cheonan, where walnut cakes are famous.

5
00:00:09,010 --> 00:00:10,680
- Toast. - I love bungeoppang so much.

6
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:12,480
- Red bean or custard? - Red bean, obviously.

7
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:14,250
- I'm custard. - Custard, obviously.

8
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:16,220
- Are you out of your mind? - She has got a subtle spark.

9
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:18,890
- A quirky side. - Yes.

10
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:20,560
Well, for Osaka, honestly...

11
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:22,630
- It has to be Takoyaki. - I think Som tam is...

12
00:00:22,630 --> 00:00:23,660
the king of food.

13
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:26,400
China is truly a powerhouse of street food.

14
00:00:26,630 --> 00:00:29,130
Dabing, youtiao, douhua, doujiang.

15
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,100
How about splitting into teams later and having a battle?

16
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,270
- A cooking battle? - Sounds good. Ready to go?

17
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:35,710
- Are we going? Ready to go? - Ready to go?

18
00:00:35,770 --> 00:00:37,410
- Ready to go? - Aren't you on the same team?

19
00:00:39,510 --> 00:00:40,610
As for me, last week's...

20
00:00:40,910 --> 00:00:43,310
dish that Jaemin and Chenle made is still...

21
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:45,350
- It was so delicious. - I still remember it.

22
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:47,290
- It's unforgettable. - I know, right?

23
00:00:47,350 --> 00:00:48,350
It was so good.

24
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:51,590
(The First Special Recipe)

25
00:00:51,890 --> 00:00:52,890
It looks perfect.

26
00:00:53,690 --> 00:00:54,990
It's pretty.

27
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:56,660
It turned out great.

28
00:00:57,060 --> 00:00:59,230
(Serving Stir-fried Tomato and Egg, Serving Savory Braised Kimchi)

29
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:03,400
By the way, today as well, there's an empty seat.

30
00:01:03,470 --> 00:01:05,300
Right here. Right next to me.

31
00:01:05,370 --> 00:01:07,210
Does this mean a new face is joining us today?

32
00:01:07,310 --> 00:01:08,310
Welcome.

33
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:09,970
- Come on out. Welcome. - Welcome.

34
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:12,010
Hello, hello.

35
00:01:13,510 --> 00:01:15,410
(The new member is Sung Hanbin of ZEROBASEONE.)

36
00:01:15,810 --> 00:01:17,120
(Full of bright energy right from his entrance)

37
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:18,950
- Welcome to... - Thank you for having me.

38
00:01:20,650 --> 00:01:23,390
- It's so great to meet you. - Yes, since I'm the first guest...

39
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:24,990
- That's right. That's right. - You really are.

40
00:01:24,990 --> 00:01:28,430
- Welcome to IDOL'S TABLE. - Let me introduce myself. Hello.

41
00:01:28,490 --> 00:01:30,600
I am Sung Hanbin, the leader of ZEROBASEONE.

42
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:34,800
I came back with my solo album.

43
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,770
(Congratulations on your solo debut.)

44
00:01:36,770 --> 00:01:38,000
Congratulations on your solo debut.

45
00:01:38,370 --> 00:01:39,440
With this solo release,

46
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:41,470
what side of Sung Hanbin do you want people to see?

47
00:01:41,710 --> 00:01:43,880
In short, "He killed the performance."

48
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:45,310
- That kind of... - "He killed the performance?"

49
00:01:46,610 --> 00:01:48,350
(He's ready to kill it today on IDOL'S TABLE.)

50
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:49,750
Then, should we start by...

51
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:51,980
exploring our new member Hanbin's tastes?

52
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:54,920
- Sounds good. - Please reveal...

53
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:57,160
- Sung Hanbin's table. - Let's go.

54
00:01:57,290 --> 00:01:58,390
Let's see your drawing skills.

55
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:01,060
- My drawing skills? - It's okay. It's okay.

56
00:02:01,130 --> 00:02:04,000
- It really is a complete mess. - No, it's fine.

57
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:05,400
Why does everyone draw so small?

58
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:07,100
I can barely see the drawing.

59
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:13,200
(Hanbin's foods drawn with lots of care)

60
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:14,910
Can you see it over there, by any chance?

61
00:02:15,010 --> 00:02:16,270
Wait a minute. What did he write there?

62
00:02:16,710 --> 00:02:18,680
Dishes I'd like to try eating...

63
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:20,250
with fans. Things like that are on here.

64
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:22,110
- That is right. - We have to guess this.

65
00:02:22,210 --> 00:02:23,850
- Let us try guessing first. - Spring rolls?

66
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:24,920
That must be pork belly.

67
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:26,580
- No. - Shabu-shabu.

68
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:29,090
No. I will give you just one hint. It is on an iron plate.

69
00:02:29,090 --> 00:02:31,990
(Q. What's on the iron plate? Try guessing along, Viewers.)

70
00:02:32,290 --> 00:02:34,090
- On an iron plate... - Are those scallions?

71
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:35,660
- Right. These are scallions. - Scallions?

72
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:36,730
And this is rice cake.

73
00:02:36,730 --> 00:02:39,400
- Rice cake? - BamBam. Spicy stir-fried chicken.

74
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:40,470
That is right.

75
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:43,770
(Artist Hanbin's profound world of art)

76
00:02:43,770 --> 00:02:46,000
- No way. - How did you know?

77
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,610
- You are a genius. - I eat it often.

78
00:02:47,610 --> 00:02:48,640
(Q. Food you want to eat with fans?)

79
00:02:49,510 --> 00:02:51,110
- That looks so delicious. - Right.

80
00:02:51,610 --> 00:02:55,010
There is a story behind it. During night study in high school,

81
00:02:55,210 --> 00:02:56,480
I really craved spicy stir-fried chicken.

82
00:02:56,610 --> 00:02:59,550
So I boldly went up to the teacher and honestly asked...

83
00:02:59,550 --> 00:03:00,920
if we could go out for it...

84
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:03,020
just for one day, but the answer was no.

85
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:04,660
Of course the answer was no.

86
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:07,630
So I thought, "Well, nothing I can do."

87
00:03:07,630 --> 00:03:08,760
But then,

88
00:03:08,830 --> 00:03:10,190
the teacher suddenly excused all of us...

89
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:11,930
from night study to let everyone eat it...

90
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,970
- Everyone? - in a group.

91
00:03:14,030 --> 00:03:17,500
Because that memory was so sweet to me,

92
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:20,100
I wanted to eat it together.

93
00:03:20,610 --> 00:03:22,570
Wait, you were born in 2001.

94
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:26,980
Kids these days have comfort foods like malatang or things like that,

95
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:28,180
but for the generation slightly above them,

96
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:29,210
they say it was spicy stir-fried chicken.

97
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:31,580
- I heard there was a culture... - Spicy stir-fried chicken.

98
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:32,780
of hanging out and eating spicy stir-fried chicken in groups.

99
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:34,390
Then what did the generation before that eat?

100
00:03:34,550 --> 00:03:35,550
They ate things like tteokbokki.

101
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:37,420
- Right? - The kind you make yourself.

102
00:03:37,490 --> 00:03:38,490
That is the best. Yes.

103
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:41,260
That actually...

104
00:03:41,330 --> 00:03:42,990
- ties into today's theme as well. - That is nice.

105
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:44,800
It is Street Food.

106
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:47,700
- Street food. - Swag.

107
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:51,000
We also have a dress code matching the concept today.

108
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:53,200
We dressed up a bit in street fashion.

109
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:55,740
You look just like our high school gym teacher.

110
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:57,110
(Laughing)

111
00:03:57,110 --> 00:03:59,180
(A physique that works in Korea, China, and Japan)

112
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:00,810
- Exactly. - Gather around!

113
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:03,380
- Exactly. - How do I look? Look at me.

114
00:04:03,450 --> 00:04:05,650
- I dressed up in a street style. - You look cool.

115
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:07,320
- I feel like I need to catch here. - It seems like you must dance.

116
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:11,190
Gil Eun Ji is back after a long time.

117
00:04:11,690 --> 00:04:13,960
- Come here, come here. - Coach. Coach.

118
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:18,230
- Coach. Coach. - Come here. The gym teacher.

119
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:20,700
I am going for that modern street look. You know what I mean?

120
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:21,700
Modern street.

121
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:24,300
(Bold ripped jeans)

122
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,110
You went all out on the lower half.

123
00:04:27,170 --> 00:04:29,340
(Flexing chains with a tough vibe)

124
00:04:29,410 --> 00:04:30,980
It feels like Jaemin is truly...

125
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:33,610
- wearing as a street vibe. - Me? But for me...

126
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:36,480
This is way too old-school...

127
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:38,180
- That is from our era. - Exactly.

128
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:39,420
Mine is similar to his.

129
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:41,150
- What is it? - That is true.

130
00:04:41,150 --> 00:04:42,150
I want to do it again too.

131
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:44,160
Lately, Y2K has made a bit of a comeback.

132
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:45,290
Y2K is back.

133
00:04:46,390 --> 00:04:48,560
In my opinion,

134
00:04:48,630 --> 00:04:49,690
Carmen's focal point is her earrings.

135
00:04:49,690 --> 00:04:50,700
- Exactly. - No.

136
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:55,000
It is not the earrings. It is me.

137
00:04:55,770 --> 00:04:57,500
(Heart-throbbing warning for older girls)

138
00:04:57,600 --> 00:04:59,240
(Rmenping's cuteness overload)

139
00:04:59,570 --> 00:05:00,740
Hey!

140
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:01,740
She crushed it.

141
00:05:01,810 --> 00:05:02,970
I had no idea Carmen had this side to her.

142
00:05:03,910 --> 00:05:06,340
(A sudden punchline from the youngest)

143
00:05:06,410 --> 00:05:08,210
Nice, nice. She has got a subtle spark.

144
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:09,950
- Subtle... Yes. - A quirky side.

145
00:05:09,950 --> 00:05:11,550
- A quirky side? - Yes.

146
00:05:11,550 --> 00:05:13,350
(5th gen's confidence acknowledged by 3rd gen)

147
00:05:13,950 --> 00:05:15,590
For our friends from abroad,

148
00:05:15,590 --> 00:05:19,020
did Korean street food look a bit different to you?

149
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:21,190
Did you ever feel like it was quite exotic or unique?

150
00:05:21,490 --> 00:05:25,600
The people selling tteokbokki felt warm, like parents.

151
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:29,000
Saying things like, "Eat a lot."

152
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:30,600
That's so touching.

153
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:33,710
So that kind of warm atmosphere felt really nice to me.

154
00:05:34,110 --> 00:05:35,810
Was there any food that felt unusual?

155
00:05:35,910 --> 00:05:39,010
I do not like rice cakes. I only ever buy lungs.

156
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:40,750
- Lungs? - Lungs in the blood sausage.

157
00:05:40,810 --> 00:05:42,780
- Lungs in the blood sausage? - I do not eat sausage, just lungs.

158
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:45,320
- You only eat lungs? - Yes, I dip those lungs in salt.

159
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:47,990
Not even the liver, just lungs?

160
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:49,720
- Yes. - What about fish cake broth?

161
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:51,490
I do not really eat fish cake either.

162
00:05:51,590 --> 00:05:53,590
Instead, the radish inside the fish cake soup...

163
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:55,390
Me too.

164
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:56,860
That is seriously the best.

165
00:05:57,360 --> 00:05:58,360
It is so delicious.

166
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:01,200
However, the radish in street-cart fish cake soup,

167
00:06:01,530 --> 00:06:03,230
- you are not supposed to eat that. - Why?

168
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:05,770
- You cannot eat that. - Well, because that radish...

169
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:07,410
is kept there just to brew the broth...

170
00:06:07,410 --> 00:06:08,410
That radish is for brewing the broth...

171
00:06:08,410 --> 00:06:09,610
Doesn't that make it even tastier?

172
00:06:09,910 --> 00:06:11,140
You should not just grab and eat that.

173
00:06:11,210 --> 00:06:12,980
Well, sometimes they will cut a piece off...

174
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,780
- and offer you a taste. - Sure, they offer it sometimes,

175
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:17,380
usually when they are swapping out the radish.

176
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:18,780
I always ask for it.

177
00:06:18,850 --> 00:06:20,450
- Really? - Yes.

178
00:06:20,450 --> 00:06:22,790
Being able to drink as much fish cake broth...

179
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:24,290
as you want was so fascinating to me.

180
00:06:25,390 --> 00:06:28,560
Well, it is basically customary to walk in when it is cold...

181
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:30,800
and start sipping broth like a welcome drink.

182
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:33,060
- A welcome drink. - I see.

183
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:34,070
I am learning so much today.

184
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:36,330
They would probably like that, right?

185
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:38,800
When I first came to Korea to become a trainee,

186
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:40,770
I went to Myeongdong a lot...

187
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:42,210
because they have so much variety there.

188
00:06:42,610 --> 00:06:45,510
- There is that tornado potato, - Tornado potato.

189
00:06:45,910 --> 00:06:47,380
and there is one thing where they explain it so well.

190
00:06:47,450 --> 00:06:49,810
There is a glass display, and he goes from one,

191
00:06:49,910 --> 00:06:53,420
then suddenly he goes 2, 3, 120, and 3.

192
00:06:54,350 --> 00:06:55,350
One hundred twenty.

193
00:06:56,390 --> 00:06:58,920
- There goes 6,284. - There goes 11,600.

194
00:06:58,990 --> 00:07:00,990
- You know that one. - Dragon's beard candy.

195
00:07:01,490 --> 00:07:02,490
How do you know that so well?

196
00:07:02,690 --> 00:07:04,960
(Impressed as the candy strands multiply)

197
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:06,900
Watching it made me so curious.

198
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:09,100
(That exact scene)

199
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:11,600
(Just like BamBam showed, 4 strands turn into 1,024.)

200
00:07:11,670 --> 00:07:14,010
Two, three, one hundred twenty.

201
00:07:14,010 --> 00:07:16,810
(Wrap honey strands around nuts to complete Dragon's beard candy)

202
00:07:16,870 --> 00:07:19,580
(Bambam's pick: An unmissable street food performance)

203
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:21,580
Honestly, my absolute favorite,

204
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:23,350
the street food you cannot find in Thailand...

205
00:07:23,410 --> 00:07:26,680
or anywhere else, it has to be on a bridge.

206
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:27,690
(Are you saying that one?)

207
00:07:27,690 --> 00:07:29,690
- Beondegi. - Beondegi.

208
00:07:29,990 --> 00:07:31,690
Beondegi. Right, right.

209
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:32,860
They rarely have it these days, though.

210
00:07:33,190 --> 00:07:35,230
There was always an older man on the Cheonggyecheon bridge,

211
00:07:35,290 --> 00:07:37,530
- on every single bridge. - Really?

212
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:38,900
These days, it is completely different.

213
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:40,900
They sell fire omelets,

214
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,600
and they serve it like a high-end restaurant.

215
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:45,000
It has changed.

216
00:07:45,770 --> 00:07:47,040
Surprisingly,

217
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:48,770
that fire omelet did not originate in Korea.

218
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:51,840
- Really? - A country in Southeast Asia...

219
00:07:51,910 --> 00:07:54,380
started doing it first,

220
00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:56,550
and then it made its way back here,

221
00:07:56,550 --> 00:07:58,680
- It made its way back here? - and people started selling it.

222
00:07:58,780 --> 00:08:00,480
Then our true street food has to be Beondegi.

223
00:08:00,550 --> 00:08:02,750
(Beondegi is the original Korean street food.)

224
00:08:02,850 --> 00:08:04,260
- Beondegi. - If you look at it, it is Beondegi.

225
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:05,320
It is the best.

226
00:08:05,890 --> 00:08:08,660
Tsuki, I heard you also have a favorite street food?

227
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:11,700
I really do. Toast.

228
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:13,200
(Agreeing)

229
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:15,600
- The one made with an egg omelet... - Yes, that is right.

230
00:08:15,670 --> 00:08:17,400
Let us hear Tsuki talk about it.

231
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:18,770
I just wanted to explain it.

232
00:08:19,970 --> 00:08:24,580
Around 5am, the vendor always shows up.

233
00:08:24,780 --> 00:08:27,450
They make it with cabbage, egg, and cheese,

234
00:08:27,450 --> 00:08:30,280
- and it has such a distinct taste. - It really does.

235
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:34,450
- And they wrap it in aluminum foil. - Right.

236
00:08:34,450 --> 00:08:36,590
I kind of loved that too.

237
00:08:36,690 --> 00:08:38,790
Strangely, street food wrapped in aluminum foil...

238
00:08:38,890 --> 00:08:40,460
somehow tastes even better.

239
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:41,690
- So true. - Exactly.

240
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:43,460
And for some reason, Carmen,

241
00:08:43,530 --> 00:08:45,100
I feel like you would love things like hotteok.

242
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:46,300
I love bungeoppang so much.

243
00:08:46,830 --> 00:08:48,370
- Bungeoppang. - Bungeoppang.

244
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:51,400
When I was a trainee, before going to my agency,

245
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:53,370
I always bought them in secret and ate them at the agency.

246
00:08:53,770 --> 00:08:55,870
In secret? Why? Do you get in trouble if you eat openly?

247
00:08:55,940 --> 00:08:57,010
I'll get in trouble right away.

248
00:08:57,010 --> 00:08:58,340
They'll say, "Are you out of your mind?"

249
00:08:58,340 --> 00:09:01,380
(Laughing)

250
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:04,980
- "Aren't you watching your diet?" - I see. It is that strict.

251
00:09:05,750 --> 00:09:07,220
- Red bean or custard? - Always...

252
00:09:07,290 --> 00:09:08,350
Red bean or custard?

253
00:09:08,590 --> 00:09:09,690
(What will they choose between...)

254
00:09:09,690 --> 00:09:10,690
(red bean bungeoppang and custard bungeoppang?)

